\description{
Published U.S. demographic rates from around 2000, prepared as input tables
for Hammel's process constructors. They give realistic examples without
downloading rates. All rates are per person-year, and ages are in years,
with half-open bands \verb{[age_start, age_end)}. Each source is a U.S. federal
statistical publication. Source files, preparation scripts and further
notes are in the package repository under
\verb{validation/data/usa_2000_2001/}; \code{data-raw/usa_2000.R} builds the
datasets.
}
\section{Mortality}{

\code{usa_2000_mortality} gives death rates by sex for ages 0-1, 1-5, five-year
bands to 100, and an open 100+ band, for use with \code{\link[=mortality]{mortality()}}. Rates are
abridged from the single-year U.S. decennial life tables for 1999-2001 as
life-table deaths divided by person-years lived in each band (\code{l100 / T100} for 100+). Under Hammel's constant hazards within bands,
\code{\link[=calculate_life_table]{calculate_life_table()}} gives life expectancy at birth of 74.05 years for
men and 79.38 for women, against the source's 74.13 and 79.47.
}

\section{Fertility}{

\code{usa_2000_fertility} gives all-women birth rates for 2000 in five-year
bands from 15 to 45, for use with \code{\link[=fertility]{fertility()}}. The published 10-14 and
45-49 rates are omitted, so the schedule sums to 2.049 births per woman
rather than the published total fertility rate of 2.056.

\code{usa_2000_fertility_union} gives the same bands by \code{union_type}, mapping
legally married women to \code{"partnered"} and unmarried women to
\code{"unpartnered"}. Legal marital status does not distinguish cohabitation.
The 40-45 rates divide births to women 40 and older by women 40-44.
}

\section{Marriage}{

\code{usa_2000_marriage} gives female marriage rates in five-year bands from 15
to 70 and an open 70+ band, for rate-based initiation in
\code{\link[=union_formation]{union_formation()}}. They are the published annual proportions of
unmarried women who married in the year before a 2001 survey, used
directly as search rates per person-year without conversion to hazards.
Marriages include first marriages and remarriages. Search attempts can fail
to find a partner, so simulated marriage rates need not match these.
}

\section{Migration}{

\code{usa_2000_migration} gives approximate migration rates between the four
census regions (\code{"NE"} Northeast, \code{"MW"} Midwest, \code{"S"} South, \code{"W"} West)
by age, March 1999 to March 2000, for \code{\link[=group_transition]{group_transition()}} on a region
dimension with these categories. Each origin-destination rate is the
all-ages flow divided by the origin's Census 2000 population, scaled by
the national age pattern of interstate moves. The same age pattern is
applied to every pair, which understates older-age moves to the South.
The rates apply to individuals; see \code{\link[=household]{household()}} for moving family
members together.
}

